Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{16}$ and then at $x=\frac{7 \pi}{8}$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{7 \pi}{8}\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}\,\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{7 \pi}{4}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{1}{2}\,\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\\&=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
$$

